 17592186045417] [17592186111742 #edb/ref 1061 8768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111742 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8bbc417-4a3f-577b-991f-4a7306fb343e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111742 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111743 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111743 #edb/ref 1061 8772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111743 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ad48197-e2e5-58b1-af58-7632d0bd7fe9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111743 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111744 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111744 #edb/ref 1061 8773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111744 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a60a9ec-5f67-5382-8520-b964b945c556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111744 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111840 #edb/ref 1064 "When solving problems with angles, it is helpful to remember the following classifications:\n\n- a **null** angle has a measure of $0^\\circ,$\n\n- an **acute** angle has a measure between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ,$\n\n- a **right** angle has a measure of $90^\\circ,$\n\n- an **obtuse** angle has a measure between $90^\\circ$ and $180^\\circ,$\n\n- a **straight** angle has a measure of $180^\\circ,$\n\n- a **reflex** angle has a measure between $180^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ,$ and\n\n- a **full** angle has a measure of $360^\\circ.$\n\nIt is also helpful to remember that when two lines meet at a point, the opposite angles are called **vertical angles**. Vertical angles are congruent, and consequently they have the same measure." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111840 #edb/ref 1065 2182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111840 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "282e8601-d3f4-5c34-a96b-a071a96de244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111840 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111922 #edb/ref 1064 "Two angles are **supplementary** if their measures add up to $180^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/88/886f1ce19ad595b470d5ad13943b780bd5a22ee737908d9554e97bd08c9ce985.png)\n\nThese angles are supplementary because the sum of their measures is $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\n105^\\circ +75^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nA **linear pair** is a pair of adjacent angles formed when two lines intersect. The angles shown above form a linear pair.\n\nThe **linear pair postulate** states the following:\n\nIf two angles form a linear pair, then they are supplementary.\n\nThe converse is *not* true. That is, if two angles are supplementary, they do not necessarily form a linear pair.\n\nTo see why, consider the diagram shown below:\n\n![](images/29/29ff0dfefb0b535342f4e8060b37fb2404983fc150681bb02d18fa6e5b00f899.png)\n\nAlthough the angles are supplementary (the sum of their measures is $120^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 180^\\circ$), they do not form a linear pair of angles since they are not adjacent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111922 #edb/ref 1065 9518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111922 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ea9626ef-f56f-5668-8f47-81a9f68c9db4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111922 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192742 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192742 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192742 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192742 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Alternate Interior Angles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192742 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192742 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d4b0977a-6bcb-5d7f-bec8-b10bff6d7e23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193385 #edb/ref 17592186045417